    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Lawrence Butler** (73.62) and **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (73.26) represent our highest-ceiling plays, though **Tyler Freeman** (71.19) rounds out a trio of hitters we are prioritizing to exploit stolen base opportunities. **Lawrence Butler** benefits immensely from a soft matchup against Jack Kochanowicz (5.20 xERA) and a massive +13% platoon adjustment, positioning him as an elite play despite a projected bottom-of-the-order assignment. Meanwhile, **Tyler Freeman** is a focal point for our SB-weighted strategy; he faces an opponent with a \"slow delivery\" flag, and when paired with a park factor of +14%, he becomes a high-leverage start. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains a staple at #2, providing high-floor production that keeps our offense stabilized regardless of the variable surrounding lineup confirmation.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Masyn Winn**, who finished with a 46.73, narrowly missing the cut behind the more efficient platoon advantages offered by **Bryson Stott** and **Drew Gilbert**. While **Masyn Winn** has the speed profile we covet, the model favored the matchup for **Drew Gilbert** against Merrill Kelly, specifically accounting for the +7% platoon boost and the same \"slow delivery\" stealing incentive that elevated **Matt Chapman** and **Tyler Freeman**. We are monitoring the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Will Smith**, **Daulton Varsho**, **Lawrence Butler**, and **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** closely; should any of them be moved down the order or benched, we have enough depth to pivot, though the absence of **Cal Raleigh** (IL) limits our flexibility at the catcher position. We are proceeding with **Bryson Stott** despite his marginal 50.45 score, as his exposure against a lefty is partially offset by a favorable park factor, though he remains our primary candidate to be pulled for a pinch hitter if the game flow stalls.",
